# Every pull request that changes what a student sees appends one line to the
# patch notes, always at the same place, so N concurrent branches would mean
# N-1 conflicts over independent prose. Union keeps both sides' lines rather
# than conflicting, which is what you want for a list of sentences.
#
# The notes are Markdown (#565) precisely so this is safe: a bullet is a whole
# line with no punctuation joining it to its neighbours, so two appends cannot
# merge into something malformed. They used to be a TypeScript array, where the
# same union merge produced a syntax error unless every entry carried a
# trailing comma -- a rule that had to be remembered on every pull request.
#
# Still load-bearing: nothing may depend on the order of notes within an entry,
# because a union merge decides it.
#
# Union never reports a conflict, which for code would be dangerous. Hence one
# path rather than a glob.
#
# This is a rule for your git, and GitHub's own merge does not read it. Landing
# a batch of pull requests that each append a note therefore needs a local
# `git merge origin/main` on every one after the first; see docs/releasing.md.
patch-notes.md merge=union
